    description: "For a tall runner who wants hardware rather than an ecosystem, this is the obvious machine, and up to a point it is an excellent one. The 60-inch belt gives 58 inches usable, which is within half an inch of the NordicTrack, and the frame is heavier than the price suggests — it was entirely stable through our 10 mph blocks. There is no account, no app and no annual fee, which for many buyers settles the question. The limitation is width. At 20 inches our 6'0\" and 6'2\" testers were comfortable and our 6'5\" tester was clipping the rails several times per run, which is not dangerous but is exactly the sort of persistent irritation that ends up mattering. The fixed screen angle is a second, smaller version of the same problem. Below about 6'3\" this is the value pick on the page; above it, buy width.",
  },
  {
    id: "life-fitness-t3",
    name: "Life Fitness T3",
    category: "Best for High Mileage",
    badge: "Longest Lasting",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Life%20Fitness%20T3+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: "/life-fitness-t3-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$3,199-$3,999",
    specs: "60 in. belt / 59 in. usable · 22 in. wide · commercial AC motor · 0-15%",
    pros: [
      "Best usable-length figure in the test at 59 inches",
      "Frame did not move at any speed any tester could run",
      "Deck feel identical at mile one and mile forty",
      "Commercial AC motor with no heat build-up on long sessions",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Roughly double the NordicTrack for advantages most runners feel only over years",
      "Does not fold and needs a permanent, planned location",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The machine that stops feeling like a compromise above 6'4\". Justifiable on high mileage, hard to justify below it.",
    description: "Commercial machines are built around users of every size, and for a tall runner that shows up immediately in the numbers: 59 inches of usable deck was the best figure we recorded, and the full 22-inch width meant no rail strikes for any tester. The frame is simply immovable — our 6'5\" tester ran 10 mph blocks on it with no detectable movement at the console, which no mid-range machine here matched. The AC motor does not build the heat that DC units develop on long sessions, and deck feel was unchanged from the first mile to the fortieth. The counterargument is straightforward: it costs roughly twice the NordicTrack, and on any single run most runners would struggle to name the difference. What they would notice is year seven. For a tall runner covering 40 miles a week, that arithmetic works.",
  },
  {
    id: "matrix-t7xe",
    name: "Matrix Fitness T7xe",
    category: "Best Deck Feel",
    badge: "Best Cushioning",
